(5584486, 806797, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>First, I generally try to play "guess the score" - I''d have put this at about a 5.6 or so.  So I''m a bit befuddled by your score.  I suspect it has to do with the flower at the top, but I''ll get to why I kinda like that in a second.<br/> <br/>Definitely meets the challenge; good focus, good depth of field, nice use of the natural light, and good detail, particularly the little hair thingies.  The flower at the top, to me, shows what is to come, and I like it there.  The OOF nature of that bloom, however, is probably not pleasing to many, and considered "distracting" by some.  The overall composition is a little plain, but it does provide some intrigue as I think there''s some detail of the plant''s leaf in the bokeh.... <br/> <br/>Is this a close view of a geranium?  (I''ve been trying to figure out what it is, and I really don''t know flowers very well.)<br/> <br/>At any rate, not sure if this has been helpful or not, but hopefully it''s been at least a little entertaining.  :-)</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! First, I generally try to play "guess the score" - I''d have put this at about a 5.6 or so.  So I''m a bit befuddled by your score.  I suspect it has to do with the flower at the top, but I''ll get to why I kinda like that in a second. Definitely meets the challenge; good focus, good depth of field, nice use of the natural light, and good detail, particularly the little hair thingies.  The flower at the top, to me, shows what is to come, and I like it there.  The OOF nature of that bloom, however, is probably not pleasing to many, and considered "distracting" by some.  The overall composition is a little plain, but it does provide some intrigue as I think there''s some detail of the plant''s leaf in the bokeh....  Is this a close view of a geranium?  (I''ve been trying to figure out what it is, and I really don''t know flowers very well.) At any rate, not sure if this has been helpful or not, but hopefully it''s been at least a little entertaining.  :-)', '2009-07-23 17:22:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(5584502, 806256, 30982, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club,<br/> <br/>Hi Kathe!  I''m pleased to have drawn this entry, as I am not that familiar with your work and this prompted me to go take a look at your portfolio - I will be back by there shortly - you have some lovely stuff!<br/> <br/>This got a 7 from me in the challenge.  I love the gentle light here, and the detail you''ve caught with the dew, particularly on the stem.  Colors work well together, and this is a nicely different view of the bloom.  The brightest section might be just a tad too bright on some monitors.  One thing you could try is a duplicate layer with multiply blending mode, then scaled back in opacity and "erased" from those areas it makes too dark.<br/> <br/>A very lovely entry - look forward to seeing more of your work in the future!  Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club, Hi Kathe!  I''m pleased to have drawn this entry, as I am not that familiar with your work and this prompted me to go take a look at your portfolio - I will be back by there shortly - you have some lovely stuff! This got a 7 from me in the challenge.  I love the gentle light here, and the detail you''ve caught with the dew, particularly on the stem.  Colors work well together, and this is a nicely different view of the bloom.  The brightest section might be just a tad too bright on some monitors.  One thing you could try is a duplicate layer with multiply blending mode, then scaled back in opacity and "erased" from those areas it makes too dark. A very lovely entry - look forward to seeing more of your work in the future!  Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions.', '2009-07-23 17:32:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(5584526, 806179, 30982, '<td valign="top">LOL!  I just drew you from the Critique Club...  I''ve already commented on this twice, though, and you know I like it...<br/> <br/>OK.  Got an 8 from me.  I loved the slight raggedness of the bloom, and as I mentioned before, the gentleness this conveys to me.  I, being me, of course love the monochromatic nature (flowers can be quite lovely in monochrome, says me.)  <br/> <br/>So why the 5.5?  Your commenters provide a bit of a clue - some people hate borders, some what deeper depth of field, and some, though I simply cannot understand them, don''t like flowers in monochrome.  Oh, and you''ve gone and allowed grain in the photo.  What were you thinking?  :-)<br/> <br/>Anyway, hope this provides a bit of insight.  If you have any questions, please don''t hesitate to PM me.</td>', 'LOL!  I just drew you from the Critique Club...  I''ve already commented on this twice, though, and you know I like it... OK.  Got an 8 from me.  I loved the slight raggedness of the bloom, and as I mentioned before, the gentleness this conveys to me.  I, being me, of course love the monochromatic nature (flowers can be quite lovely in monochrome, says me.)   So why the 5.5?  Your commenters provide a bit of a clue - some people hate borders, some what deeper depth of field, and some, though I simply cannot understand them, don''t like flowers in monochrome.  Oh, and you''ve gone and allowed grain in the photo.  What were you thinking?  :-) Anyway, hope this provides a bit of insight.  If you have any questions, please don''t hesitate to PM me.', '2009-07-23 17:49:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(5584789, 806762, 100773, '<td valign="top">Never underestimate point and shoot cameras! Beautifull photo!</td>', 'Never underestimate point and shoot cameras! Beautifull photo!', '2009-07-23 21:14:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(5584822, 806756, 69246, '<td valign="top">** Hello from the Critique Club **<br/> <br/>My comments are broken down into technical and composition sections.<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>First, I like the way you concentrated on complementary colors. The bright orange/yellow is complemented well with the green background. This provides a bold, excited photograph, as intended by your subject and title "Fire Lily". You also have the flower along 1/3 lines which offers a pleasing proportion. The flower extends from the upper right to the lower left leaving the stamens along the right third. However, the angle from where you are shooting directs the opening of the flower in a parallel plane to the background, this tends to flatten the image. <br/> <br/>If the flower was angled so that the stamens was pointed more toward the viewer it would add an additional dimensional plane, as well as expose more of the center of the flower.<br/> <br/>You did a good job of isolating the flower from the background although a little more blur would help separate the flower even more.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>The main problem is that any flower shot, especially ones with macros need to be sharp, razor sharp. The lens you were using was  a 70 - 200 mm lens so I''m surprised you used macro tubes. Personally, I have not seen very good macro tubes, teleconverters, etc and I have always experienced image degradation from them. <br/> <br/>When shooting, did you shoot this free hand? The best flower shots are performed on a tripod or monopod. If it is windy, a monopod may be better since you can move with more freedom than with a tripod.<br/> <br/>At f/11, there should be enough DOF to get a sharp image. I have quite a few questions, so feel free to answer and I can comment more. How far were you from the subject? How much of the flower took up the viewfinder? How much did you crop?<br/> <br/>If you have any questions or would like to discuss further, please feel free to send me a PM.<br/> <br/></td>', '** Hello from the Critique Club ** My comments are broken down into technical and composition sections. Composition First, I like the way you concentrated on complementary colors. The bright orange/yellow is complemented well with the green background. This provides a bold, excited photograph, as intended by your subject and title "Fire Lily". You also have the flower along 1/3 lines which offers a pleasing proportion. The flower extends from the upper right to the lower left leaving the stamens along the right third. However, the angle from where you are shooting directs the opening of the flower in a parallel plane to the background, this tends to flatten the image.  If the flower was angled so that the stamens was pointed more toward the viewer it would add an additional dimensional plane, as well as expose more of the center of the flower. You did a good job of isolating the flower from the background although a little more blur would help separate the flower even more. Technical The main problem is that any flower shot, especially ones with macros need to be sharp, razor sharp. The lens you were using was  a 70 - 200 mm lens so I''m surprised you used macro tubes. Personally, I have not seen very good macro tubes, teleconverters, etc and I have always experienced image degradation from them.  When shooting, did you shoot this free hand? The best flower shots are performed on a tripod or monopod. If it is windy, a monopod may be better since you can move with more freedom than with a tripod. At f/11, there should be enough DOF to get a sharp image. I have quite a few questions, so feel free to answer and I can comment more. How far were you from the subject? How much of the flower took up the viewfinder? How much did you crop? If you have any questions or would like to discuss further, please feel free to send me a PM. ', '2009-07-23 21:26:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(5584989, 806739, 69246, '<td valign="top">** Hello from the Critique Club ** <br/> <br/>First, very nice shot.<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>The use of complementary colors is a very prominent aspect of the composition of this photograph: blue and orange, violet and green. The placement of the center of the flower on the lower right 1/3 point adds proportionality to the photo. The very shallow DOF isolates the flower center well. The only issue with the DOF is that the leftmost petal falls right in the middle of the DOF plane making it seem slightly out of focus, even though this is due to DOF. With the angle and lens it is understandable how difficult of a shot this was. I also do like the tricolor background: green to blue to violet as this gives a sense of movement and progression. This photo is very calming, and attribute obtained from the colors used.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Focus is sharp, which is one reason why this scored so high. Sharpness in floral macro shots is the most important technical objective. I noticed that you shot this on a bright sunny day. That was probably the most important aspect for lighting as flowers tend to look more vibrant in sunlight than artificial. With that said, I''m wondering if a little off camera fill light would have been helpful for the center of the flower. Finally, the DOF suggestions above would have been minimized by using a different angle, slightly higher and aimed down; I don''t think the composition strength would have been diminished.<br/> <br/>Again, very nice shot, certainly one worthy of its score and position.</td>', '** Hello from the Critique Club **  First, very nice shot. Composition The use of complementary colors is a very prominent aspect of the composition of this photograph: blue and orange, violet and green. The placement of the center of the flower on the lower right 1/3 point adds proportionality to the photo. The very shallow DOF isolates the flower center well. The only issue with the DOF is that the leftmost petal falls right in the middle of the DOF plane making it seem slightly out of focus, even though this is due to DOF. With the angle and lens it is understandable how difficult of a shot this was. I also do like the tricolor background: green to blue to violet as this gives a sense of movement and progression. This photo is very calming, and attribute obtained from the colors used. Technical Focus is sharp, which is one reason why this scored so high. Sharpness in floral macro shots is the most important technical objective. I noticed that you shot this on a bright sunny day. That was probably the most important aspect for lighting as flowers tend to look more vibrant in sunlight than artificial. With that said, I''m wondering if a little off camera fill light would have been helpful for the center of the flower. Finally, the DOF suggestions above would have been minimized by using a different angle, slightly higher and aimed down; I don''t think the composition strength would have been diminished. Again, very nice shot, certainly one worthy of its score and position.', '2009-07-24 00:03:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(5584999, 806302, 31637, '<td valign="top">Hello, found this in the Critique Club section:<br/> <br/>As for the challenge, it sure fits of course, but the choice of subject is less exotic than most of the flowers seen in the challenge. <br/> <br/>Technically, the photo is sound. I like the tones on the petals and the shallow depth of field works well against the rocky background which provides good contrast overall, bringing out the subject even more. The framing is ok, cropped as close as possible for a top shot of a cluster of flora. <br/> <br/>Artistically, not the most pop or wow factor given that you had the chance to take advantage of the Advanced Editing clause. Not sure what you could have done here, I''d like to see this in Black and White. <br/> <br/>I think this is one of those shots that isn''t flawed too much, but also gives a little lasting impression, at least artistically. I think it scored what it deserved, it''s a well photographed subject but lacking in drama.</td>', 'Hello, found this in the Critique Club section: As for the challenge, it sure fits of course, but the choice of subject is less exotic than most of the flowers seen in the challenge.  Technically, the photo is sound. I like the tones on the petals and the shallow depth of field works well against the rocky background which provides good contrast overall, bringing out the subject even more. The framing is ok, cropped as close as possible for a top shot of a cluster of flora.  Artistically, not the most pop or wow factor given that you had the chance to take advantage of the Advanced Editing clause. Not sure what you could have done here, I''d like to see this in Black and White.  I think this is one of those shots that isn''t flawed too much, but also gives a little lasting impression, at least artistically. I think it scored what it deserved, it''s a well photographed subject but lacking in drama.', '2009-07-24 00:16:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(5585001, 806726, 31637, '<td valign="top">Hello, found this in the critque club section:<br/> <br/>I think the photo suffered in this challenge because the main focus isn''t the flower. I think its the main reason why points were docked. <br/> <br/>Technically, shot well, the frame is filled up good, maybe a closer crop wouldn''t hurt this by giving it a more abstract feel. <br/> <br/>Artisically, very nice and not over-processed but for advanced edtiting, probably under-processed. Maybe you could have taken advantage by boosting the tones a bit. I think this is pretty creative and moody. I like the production values of the shot. But like you mentioned in the details, its out of the box, and most of these become overlooked even though there is loads of creativity. <br/> <br/>Overall, a 5.0 is a good score in a challenge like this...luckily, you didn''t fall under average.</td>', 'Hello, found this in the critque club section: I think the photo suffered in this challenge because the main focus isn''t the flower. I think its the main reason why points were docked.  Technically, shot well, the frame is filled up good, maybe a closer crop wouldn''t hurt this by giving it a more abstract feel.  Artisically, very nice and not over-processed but for advanced edtiting, probably under-processed. Maybe you could have taken advantage by boosting the tones a bit. I think this is pretty creative and moody. I like the production values of the shot. But like you mentioned in the details, its out of the box, and most of these become overlooked even though there is loads of creativity.  Overall, a 5.0 is a good score in a challenge like this...luckily, you didn''t fall under average.', '2009-07-24 00:21:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(5585018, 805522, 69246, '<td valign="top">** Hello from the Critique Club ** <br/> <br/>Wow! That is my first impression when I saw this.<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>The fact that the flower has been isolated from any background is a very important aspect to the overall composition of this photo. The flower is centered but the break in the petals on the lower right is enough to add a sense of tension to the photo. One very nice aspect is the very subtle use of color against a neutral color flower. The overall purity in color is gently offset by yellow and then red. <br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>Impressive use of light and HDR. The contrast really makes this photo appear to extend beyond the plane of the screen. I can feel the depth in the photo. Focus is key and overall this shot is nicely in focus. The lower petal is noticeably different in terms of focus, which tends to show after a longer view. I really like how the soft lighting brings out the water droplets without a "hot spot", attributed to the fact you did this on a heavily overcast day. It would be interesting to see which layers you used as this is a great example to teach HDR with. Perhaps an entry in "How did they do that" would be a nice supplement to this fantastic shot.</td>', '** Hello from the Critique Club **  Wow! That is my first impression when I saw this. Composition The fact that the flower has been isolated from any background is a very important aspect to the overall composition of this photo. The flower is centered but the break in the petals on the lower right is enough to add a sense of tension to the photo. One very nice aspect is the very subtle use of color against a neutral color flower. The overall purity in color is gently offset by yellow and then red.  Technical Impressive use of light and HDR. The contrast really makes this photo appear to extend beyond the plane of the screen. I can feel the depth in the photo. Focus is key and overall this shot is nicely in focus. The lower petal is noticeably different in terms of focus, which tends to show after a longer view. I really like how the soft lighting brings out the water droplets without a "hot spot", attributed to the fact you did this on a heavily overcast day. It would be interesting to see which layers you used as this is a great example to teach HDR with. Perhaps an entry in "How did they do that" would be a nice supplement to this fantastic shot.', '2009-07-24 00:36:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(5585054, 806675, 51638, '<td valign="top">Just going back thru recent challenges and spotted this. Whilst I can understand folks questioning the relevance of the nude in the background, I personally think the juxtaposition of the dried flowers against the smooth skin works extremely well. You were never going to win this challenge with this subject matter, but you deserved a <u>far</u> better score than 5.5.<br/></td>', 'Just going back thru recent challenges and spotted this. Whilst I can understand folks questioning the relevance of the nude in the background, I personally think the juxtaposition of the dried flowers against the smooth skin works extremely well. You were never going to win this challenge with this subject matter, but you deserved a far better score than 5.5.', '2009-07-24 01:14:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(5585474, 805297, 9114, '<td valign="top">Very nice work!<br/>Debbi</td>', 'Very nice work!Debbi', '2009-07-24 15:47:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(5585581, 806625, 16912, '<td valign="top">Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club<br/> <br/><b>First Impression:</b> Rather than being drawn to the texture of the dead flowers I''m distracted by the rather boring wall.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> The central composition isn''t doing this image any harm, then again, its not very interesting.<br/> <br/><b>Tecnical:</b> This doesn''t ''pop'' or have any ''wow'' factor for me, exposure is ok but the focus is a little soft in places<br/> <br/><b>Artistic:</b> For me this is rather bland and have much artistic value sorry.<br/> <br/><b>Overall thoughts:</b> I guess you chose this location for the nice little crack? but I think it was the wrong choice. I just glanced at your profile and saw your top 2 submissions are flowers and am sure you agree that they are so much better than this.  5.5 is a good final score I think.<br/> <br/>Get in touch if you''d like to discuss further Gennaro.</td>', 'Greetings from Andi via the Critique Club First Impression: Rather than being drawn to the texture of the dead flowers I''m distracted by the rather boring wall. Composition: The central composition isn''t doing this image any harm, then again, its not very interesting. Tecnical: This doesn''t ''pop'' or have any ''wow'' factor for me, exposure is ok but the focus is a little soft in places Artistic: For me this is rather bland and have much artistic value sorry. Overall thoughts: I guess you chose this location for the nice little crack? but I think it was the wrong choice. I just glanced at your profile and saw your top 2 submissions are flowers and am sure you agree that they are so much better than this.  5.5 is a good final score I think. Get in touch if you''d like to discuss further Gennaro.', '2009-07-24 17:57:14.000000', NULL, 0),
(5585904, 806841, 18197, '<td valign="top">This is really lovely. Congratulations on such a stunning photo.</td>', 'This is really lovely. Congratulations on such a stunning photo.', '2009-07-25 02:54:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(5586295, 806237, 51269, '<td valign="top">I gave this one of my few 7s- it''s very well done - with a dof that sings :)</td>', 'I gave this one of my few 7s- it''s very well done - with a dof that sings :)', '2009-07-25 18:50:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(5586345, 806743, 96664, '<td valign="top">This one got robbed... Should be top 10 for sure! well done!</td>', 'This one got robbed... Should be top 10 for sure! well done!', '2009-07-25 20:19:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(5586351, 806743, 12674, '<td valign="top">Thanks for your comment. It would nice to crack the top 10.  Steve</td>', 'Thanks for your comment. It would nice to crack the top 10.  Steve', '2009-07-25 20:32:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(5586360, 805999, 2553, '<td valign="top">I have to say that it has been wonderful to see this on the front page every day when I visit - it has been a real pleasure to look at, and I will miss it when its gone.  Thanks!</td>', 'I have to say that it has been wonderful to see this on the front page every day when I visit - it has been a real pleasure to look at, and I will miss it when its gone.  Thanks!', '2009-07-25 21:05:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(5586506, 806050, 62357, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Well done on getting a solid score in this challenge.<br/> <br/>I like the centered composition, it works well for this type of flower shot, as does the square crop. <br/> <br/>Having seen the original, I am very impressed with the processing you have done, definitely contributed to your score.  Had you entered the original, I don''t think you would have scored nearly as high.<br/> <br/>From a technical point of view I would really have liked this image to be a bit sharper. It feels just slightly out of focus rather than a deliberate depth of field choice.  <br/> <br/>Overall though a good choice and a decent score.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Well done on getting a solid score in this challenge. I like the centered composition, it works well for this type of flower shot, as does the square crop.  Having seen the original, I am very impressed with the processing you have done, definitely contributed to your score.  Had you entered the original, I don''t think you would have scored nearly as high. From a technical point of view I would really have liked this image to be a bit sharper. It feels just slightly out of focus rather than a deliberate depth of field choice.   Overall though a good choice and a decent score.', '2009-07-26 04:16:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(5586619, 806179, 68586, '<td valign="top">I really like this shot. The crop, the processing and the composition.<br/>Certainly it could have had a greater DoF to include the centre of the flower . . it could also have had more bighting sharpness . . but as <img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=30982" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Melethia</a> says - there is a lovely gentleness about this picture which is aided by the toning.<br/> <br/>My only nitpick is your title. For me titles are always important, (not that I''m great at them before you look) but they give the viewer an idea of what you are trying to capture and convey . . the message you want them to take away, or a context /  framework to position the image in, even just a factual description of the object. <br/> <br/>While this title may well have been intended as gently self depreciating, there is always a danger that it suggest you don''t really care - and you know what ? If the artist doesn''t really care - then why should the viewer ?  You have a good range of titles on your other shot''s and I think a shot as nice as this deserved more than ''Just another flower''   ;- )<br/> <br/></td>', 'I really like this shot. The crop, the processing and the composition.Certainly it could have had a greater DoF to include the centre of the flower . . it could also have had more bighting sharpness . . but as  Melethia says - there is a lovely gentleness about this picture which is aided by the toning. My only nitpick is your title. For me titles are always important, (not that I''m great at them before you look) but they give the viewer an idea of what you are trying to capture and convey . . the message you want them to take away, or a context /  framework to position the image in, even just a factual description of the object.  While this title may well have been intended as gently self depreciating, there is always a danger that it suggest you don''t really care - and you know what ? If the artist doesn''t really care - then why should the viewer ?  You have a good range of titles on your other shot''s and I think a shot as nice as this deserved more than ''Just another flower''   ;- ) ', '2009-07-26 13:12:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(5587013, 806820, 69246, '<td valign="top">** Hello from the Critique Club **<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>When this image first loaded on my screen I saw primarily the top white portion of the photo. Then, as I scrolled down and saw more of the flower, the compositional feel changed dramatically. This was a result of a square crop which really helped the photo out the most. The nicest part of this composition, and what really sets this from many others is that the image is not static; its not just a photo of a flower. Rather, there is distinct motion as petals (or pollen?) are floating around the flower. This really adds a sense of tranquility to the photo. The flower is nicely isolated from the background. I think a horizontal crop may have worked better with more open space to the left.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>The focus is sharp; the 4.0 aperture was definitely a good choice. There is the right amount of DOF to isolate the flower, but still have enough of the foreground elements in focus. The black and white conversion really works with this photo as this photo has elements in almost all zones. The main problem, and what probably brought the score down, was the white negative space on the top. It really doesn''t add to the photo and distracts from the central composition.</td>', '** Hello from the Critique Club ** Composition When this image first loaded on my screen I saw primarily the top white portion of the photo. Then, as I scrolled down and saw more of the flower, the compositional feel changed dramatically. This was a result of a square crop which really helped the photo out the most. The nicest part of this composition, and what really sets this from many others is that the image is not static; its not just a photo of a flower. Rather, there is distinct motion as petals (or pollen?) are floating around the flower. This really adds a sense of tranquility to the photo. The flower is nicely isolated from the background. I think a horizontal crop may have worked better with more open space to the left. Technical The focus is sharp; the 4.0 aperture was definitely a good choice. There is the right amount of DOF to isolate the flower, but still have enough of the foreground elements in focus. The black and white conversion really works with this photo as this photo has elements in almost all zones. The main problem, and what probably brought the score down, was the white negative space on the top. It really doesn''t add to the photo and distracts from the central composition.', '2009-07-26 22:19:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(5587060, 806360, 31637, '<td valign="top">Hello found this in the <u>Critique Club section</u> <br/> <br/>I can appreciate many things about this shot, and also find it a bit hard to overlook the flaws. There are many good photographers on this site who specialize in flora, and Ive learned alot from the work here to know that this shot is on par with the best, at least technically. <br/> <br/>The framing and focus are very above average and I guess a its a good choice going with that aperture setting for a shallow d.o.f. maybe a few stops down to 5.6 might give a better effect. I enjoy the particular look of this flower too, it''s great for close crops like this, lighting isn''t the best, hurting the contrast however.<br/> <br/>Now a look at some flaws; at least artistically. The color for this isn''t that vivid. It looks de-saturated a bit. Better contrast could be achieved through the color. I also think the noise distracts, maybe over-use of levels? <br/> <br/>Overall, a solid floral. I think it scored what it deserved, PM me if you have questions about this critique.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hello found this in the Critique Club section  I can appreciate many things about this shot, and also find it a bit hard to overlook the flaws. There are many good photographers on this site who specialize in flora, and Ive learned alot from the work here to know that this shot is on par with the best, at least technically.  The framing and focus are very above average and I guess a its a good choice going with that aperture setting for a shallow d.o.f. maybe a few stops down to 5.6 might give a better effect. I enjoy the particular look of this flower too, it''s great for close crops like this, lighting isn''t the best, hurting the contrast however. Now a look at some flaws; at least artistically. The color for this isn''t that vivid. It looks de-saturated a bit. Better contrast could be achieved through the color. I also think the noise distracts, maybe over-use of levels?  Overall, a solid floral. I think it scored what it deserved, PM me if you have questions about this critique. ', '2009-07-26 23:25:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(5587065, 806858, 70369, '<td valign="top">this is simply elegant &amp; gorgeous !!! The background reminds me of a water color painting.  The DOF is perfect.. I can''t believe it didn''t place higher.. One of my favorites.</td>', 'this is simply elegant & gorgeous !!! The background reminds me of a water color painting.  The DOF is perfect.. I can''t believe it didn''t place higher.. One of my favorites.', '2009-07-26 23:32:09.000000', NULL, 0),
(5588028, 806385, 49773, '<td valign="top">Mmm, sweeet!</td>', 'Mmm, sweeet!', '2009-07-27 16:18:53.000000', NULL, 0),
